1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,030
Imagine a future where our streets are filled with ads 200m tall; where billboards change before our eyes to give us personalised messages; and where companies track our every move as we walk through a city, pinging our phones with special offers as we near their shops.
想象一下这样的未来：我们的街道上充斥着高达200米的广告；广告牌在我们眼前变换，并提供个性化的信息；当我们在城市中穿行时，公司会追踪我们的一举一动，当我们靠近它们的店铺时，就会向我们的手机发送特价优惠的信息。

2
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:21,230
In fact, all of these forms of advertising are already being tested.
事实上，所有这些形式的广告都已经在测试中了。

3
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:23,680
Let‘s take the first of these ideas.
我们先来看看第一个设想。

4
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:33,330
Back in 1982, science fiction movie Blade Runner envisaged a futuristic cityscape where giant adverts stretched the full height of skyscrapers.
早在1982年，科幻电影《银翼杀手》就设想了一个未来的城市景象，在那里巨大的广告延展到整个摩天大楼的高度。

5
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:39,310
And UK company Lightvert are now doing something very similar; with one crucial difference.
英国公司Lightvert现在也在做类似的事情，不过有一个关键的区别。

6
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:46,360
Instead of making immense physical adverts, their commercials are beamed straight into consumers’ eyes.
他们没有制作巨大的实体广告，而是将广告直接投射到消费者的眼睛里。

7
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:52,960
They do this by attaching a strip of reflective material to a wall, and then projecting an image onto it.
他们通过在墙上贴一条反光材料，然后将图像投射到上面来实现这一点的。

8
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,610
The image then ‘bounces‘ out.
然后这个图像就会“反射”到外界。

9
00:00:55,610 --> 00:00:59,150
You can see it when you walk directly in front of it.
当你直接从图像前面经过时，你就能看到它。

10
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:05,860
It looks huge and suspended in mid- air, but no one else can - you can only see it from one specific spot.
它看起来巨大且悬浮在半空中，但其他人看不到——只有在一个特定的位置才能看到。

11
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:14,530
Lightvert claims this form of advertising is unobtrusive and that it can “unlock” vast amounts of “high- value advertising real estate”.
Lightvert公司声称这种形式的广告不引人注目，并且能够“解锁”大量的“高价值广告不动产”。

12
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:19,450
But not everyone is sold on the idea of living in a world of endless ads.
但并不是每个人都赞同生活在一个充斥着无尽广告的世界里。

13
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:25,270
In Moscow, for example, a new form of advertising targeting motorists has split opinion.
例如，在莫斯科，一种针对司机的的新型广告引发了意见分歧。

14
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:35,060
Drivers approaching a particular digital billboard in the city are shown adverts for a new kind of Jaguar car, but only if you are driving a different make of vehicle.
当驾驶员接近城市中某个特定的数字广告牌时，会看到一个新型捷豹汽车的广告，但前提是你驾驶的是其他品牌的车辆。

15
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:41,470
Sensors in the billboard detect what kind of car you are driving, and send you an ad based on that information.
广告牌中的传感器会检测你驾驶的汽车类型，并根据该信息向你发送广告。

16
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:47,360
While this may seem invasive, it’s actually the safety aspect that has concerned some observers.
虽然这可能看起来具有侵扰性，但实际上一些观察人士担心的是安全方面的问题。

17
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:53,490
Researchers in Sweden have shown that digital billboards attract our eyes for longer than other kinds.
瑞典的研究人员已经表明，数字广告牌吸引我们目光的时间比其他类型的广告牌更长。

18
00:01:53,490 --> 00:02:00,390
In fact, they distract us for over two seconds, a length of time which studies have proved to be dangerous on the roads.
事实上，它们会让我们在道路上分心超过两秒钟，而研究已经证明，这个时长是很危险的。

19
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:08,680
Finally, we have “proximity marketing”, where shops detect where you are on the high street and send tailored adverts to your phone.
最后，还有“近距离营销”，即商店会检测你在商业街的位置，并向你的手机发送定制的广告。

20
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:13,960
Devicescape is one company trialling the use of Wi-Fi as a means of tracking customers.
Devicescape这家公司正在试验用Wi-Fi作为追踪顾客的手段。

21
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:21,510
Their technology could work anywhere, whether in “a supermarket, a concert hall, or a bus,” says Owen Geddes from their marketing team.
他们营销团队的欧文·格迪斯表示，他们的技术可以在任何地方使用，无论是“在超市、音乐厅还是公交车上”。

22
00:02:21,510 --> 00:02:23,460
What would customers see?
顾客会看到什么呢？

23
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:31,240
“It could be a discount offer to use in the shop the person is visiting, or a piece of content such as a movie trailer,” says Mr Geddes.
格迪斯先生说：“可能是在顾客正在光顾的商店里使用的打折优惠，或者是像电影预告片这样的内容。”
